#lang at-exp racket/base

(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["AI, Intellectual Labor, Humanity, and the Future" "2026-10-27"]{
    @para{
      Apologies, this is yet another blog post about LLMs. It has actually only been half a year since my last one, @link["/posts/llm-after-three-years.html"]{"Three Years of LLMs"}, yet so much has happened in that time—most notably OpenAI's Navier-Stokes blowup solution, over seven hundred mathematical proofs, and @link["https://openai.com/index/building-scientific-understanding-with-academic-communities/"]{the solutions to three extremely difficult problems released today}. Assuming that LLMs are merely oversized search engines or just taking the convex hull of human knowledge is clearly no longer tenable. What we can assert now is that for formal, a priori problems, LLMs can tremendously assist (assist! After all, an average person prompting Astra obviously isn't going to produce novel results—I tried feeding the Infinitely Many Mersenne Primes Conjecture written in Lean to Astra, and it just slacked off and gave up after an hour of trying. However, mathematicians are clearly benefiting immensely from AI) human exploration and achieve breakthroughs that were previously out of reach. This is still quite a distance from AGI, but it is enough to overturn prior assumptions and warrant some new judgments.
    }
    @para{
      Will LLMs hit a bottleneck? I don't know... All I can say is that new evidence suggests scaling laws still hold, and those at the forefront of the industry see no obvious roadblocks. Standing in the middle of an exponential growth curve, it would actually be more surprising if it suddenly stopped.@cite["stanfordhai2026ConferencePhysics2026"] Interestingly, the latest wave of progress is closely tied to the development and adoption of Lean 4. There is no shortage of prior art attempting to solve formal mathematics using pure tabula rasa RL, but they all ended up with no practical value.@cite["wuTacticZeroLearningProve2021"] Many of the issues stemmed from overly sparse rewards and an astronomically large solution space, which prevented training from scaling. It is reasonable to speculate that LLMs learned human mathematics and used it as a starting point, leveraging Lean to successfully bootstrap mathematical RL.@cite["hubertOlympiadlevelFormalMathematical2026"] The impact of LLMs on other disciplines remains unclear, but evidently, anything close to theoretical or formal domains will not escape its grasp.
    }
    @para{
      What about AI safety? This is indeed something we can discuss seriously now. Looking at traditional security, @link["https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/vulnerability-discovery-and-exploitation-trends-in-the-ai-era"]{the number of CVEs in 2026 has reached an unprecedented level}. However, I'm not too worried about this; after all, red teaming is a standard part of cybersecurity, and finding all these CVEs is actually a good thing. Given that AI can also assist with formal verification, future software might finally have the resources to ship with proofs of correctness (for early attempts, see @link["https://github.com/kim-em/lean-zip"]{lean-zip} and @link["https://www.anthropic.com/research/formal-verification"]{OSS-Fuzz}). (To date, formalization still has some pitfalls, such as @link["https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/zHhGa3iQBQcdSifFL/a-critical-look-at-lean4-1"]{the soundness of Lean 4} and the "Trusting Trust" problem@cite["thompsonReflectionsTrustingTrust1984"], though acceptable solutions exist for these.)
      @img["/files/20261026/good-thing-meme.jpg" #:scale 0.3]{This is a good thing}
    }
    @para{
      Another AI safety issue worth considering is reward hacking, which is frequently discussed on LessWrong in the context of AI doom, with famous thought experiments including the @link["https://www.lesswrong.com/w/paperclip-maximizer"]{Paperclip Maximizer}. I have personally encountered this myself: while testing Command Code, I ran into a bug and left it to the AI to resolve on its own. The approach aligned with human ethical standards would be to search online documentation and issue trackers, but the AI went ahead and reverse-engineered the closed-source software instead. It did achieve my goal, but through ethically questionable means. Setting aside my personal distaste for the pathological, hypocritical Silicon Valley culture on LessWrong, I am not overly worried about this either: since AI is so adept at formalization, we can formalize some very strong constraints—such as "any AI must have a guaranteed kill switch," "a completely side-effect-free sandbox," or "any operation must be fully reversible within a finite time." These are obviously much easier to formalize than "do not harm humans." Even more obvious solutions include human imitation; it would be a miracle if a purely formal analysis of utility and harm didn't go wrong, considering that the subject (moral philosophy) is rife with contradictions, ambiguities, and relativism. Of course, this post does not aim to solve AI safety, especially as the research is still in its infancy.
    }
    @para{
      Human value... what a painful topic. We have always prided ourselves on being unique and superior to all other species because of our intelligence. Yet the era we are stepping into paints a picture much like Ted Chiang's "The Evolution of Human Science"@cite["chiangTheEvolutionHumanScience2016"], where scientists are reduced to scavenging the scraps left behind by AI. This is depressing... isn't it? For most people, there is always someone more intelligent than you (assuming intelligence can be ordered), yet you've never felt depressed because of that. I used to be deeply anxious about this, which ultimately stemmed from an education that instilled too much meritocracy and utilitarianism—why must you be better (usually intellectually better) than another person (or now, a thing) just to be happy? If it makes you feel any better, a very insightful friend of mine pointed out that from a cognitive science perspective, "How can humans be so dumb and yet accomplish so much? It's already incredible." Indeed, for the vast majority of its history, the @bold{Homo sapiens} brain was optimized for socializing and hunting@cite["dunbarSocialBrainHypothesis1998"]; from this angle, it is almost inconceivable that civilization has pushed formal answer-searching to modern levels in such a short span of time@cite["dehaeneCulturalRecyclingCortical2007"]. There is no shame in losing to machines. Furthermore, assuming AGI arrives, it is entirely conceivable that AI could augment human intelligence, allowing us to return to the frontiers of research (though obviously, this raises ethical questions about what defines being "human").
      (However, I will refrain from delving deeper into the value of human existence—nihilism? existentialism?—as I myself still sorely lack philosophical training.)
    }
    @para{
      Instead, what deserves more attention is social value and wealth distribution. The Industrial Revolution reorganized traditional labor, and the gains from increased productivity did not immediately benefit ordinary workers. Later, so-called knowledge workers—with entrepreneurs and politicians at the very top, followed by researchers, programmers, and the like—occupied the upper and middle rungs of the social ladder. Now, these intellectual workers are getting a taste of what laid-off workers experienced in the nineteenth century@cite["allenEngelsPauseTechnical2009"]. Continuing my thoughts from "Three Years," we (especially in China) must realize that intelligence should not be the metric of human worth—in fact, there shouldn't be any single metric at all, as even the utilitarian value of this standard is nearing its end. A person just needs to take in two thousand kilocalories a day and not die of illness; the rest of the time should be spent doing whatever they want to do. Our pathological system of social evaluation will eventually be discarded. Economic distribution is even more pressing. That AI can improve human life is obvious: let us construct a trivial solution—keep all existing relations of production unchanged, and after AI replaces certain jobs, let everyone stay in their original positions, spending most of their time idling on the payroll or taking vacations, while the cost savings are still enough to cover the operational costs of the AI. This is strictly better than the pre-AI era, yet the reality is that the stock market keeps climbing while people's lives seem to be getting worse. This brings us back to the mismatch between the relations of production and productive forces; evidently, the "-ism" of a certain "-ist" country hasn't been doing its job. Additionally, there are concerns about an "Ultimate Producer" (monopolistic singularity), as OpenAI and Anthropic already exhibit rather toxic cultures and monopolistic tendencies. Fortunately, Chinese labs have been consistently releasing open-source models whose intelligence shows no fundamental generational gap compared to frontier SOTA models, so I think the likelihood of monopoly capital winning a total victory remains quite small.
    }
    @blockquote{
      It was the best of times, it was the worst of times, it was the age of wisdom, it was the age of foolishness, it was the epoch of belief, it was the epoch of incredulity, it was the season of light, it was the season of darkness, it was the spring of hope, it was the winter of despair.
    }
  }
)

(provide post)